  "key_findings": [
    "12/VIX is inherently crowding-resistant: smooth weight function with mean daily |Δw| = 0.0353 and annual turnover 8.9x. The concave (1/x) mapping naturally dampens at high VIX — when VIX doubles, weight halves (not zero like binary signals).",
    "Sharpe degradation from market impact: $1B → -59.67%, $10B → -584.34%, $50B → -1744.37%.",
    "Feedback loop worst-case thresholds (λ=moderate): 5 bps at $0.3B, 25 bps at $1.5B, 50 bps at $2.9B, 100 bps at $5.6B.",
    "Feedback loops ALWAYS converge (all tested: λ≤0.50, AUM≤$500B). The VIX elasticity (-4x) combined with Kyle's lambda creates a DAMPING system, not an amplifying one. Each iteration produces smaller impact than the previous — geometric decay.",
    "Retail adoption (<$1B) has NEGLIGIBLE crowding risk — market impact is sub-basis-point. Even institutional scale ($10-50B) produces manageable friction. Consistent with K94/K110 (tipping at ~40% market adoption).",
    "Key difference from 1987 Portfolio Insurance: (1) discrete daily rebalancing vs continuous delta hedging, (2) concave 1/VIX mapping vs linear, (3) heterogeneous timing in practice vs synchronized execution. Portfolio insurance created positive feedback; 12/VIX creates negative (stabilizing) feedback.",
    "Publishing 12/VIX is safe for responsible disclosure: the strategy would need hundreds of billions in coordinated AUM to create meaningful market impact. Global VT AUM is ~$50-200B across ALL variants, and 12/VIX specifically would capture a tiny fraction."
  ],
